  <!-- ========== 3. DELTA SECTION ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (June 10–21, 2026)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · 11-day gap catch-up · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Islamabad Talks (April 11-12, 21 hours, named US and Iranian principals including Araghchi and Witkoff) produced no final agreement. During June 10-16, negotiations continued via document exchange through Pakistan and Qatar as relay mediators. Pakistan's PM Sharif announced June 14 that a "final agreed-upon text" had been reached, with US VP Vance confirming digital co-signature the same day. However, no TIER1-confirmed convened face-to-face or formally structured indirect session occurred before EOD June 16 — the text agreement was reached through written document relay, not a held session. Per F1's ambiguity rule (written-message exchanges that are not a convened session = AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO), F1 resolved NO. The June 17 Versailles ceremony — Trump and Pezeshkian signing the MOU remotely with Macron as G7 host — constituted the qualifying convened session with named principals, resolving F2 YES within its June 10-24 window. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The US-Iran war ran continuously from February 28 through June 17. Israeli strikes on Iranian military leadership continued June 13-14; US intervention blocked an anticipated Iranian counter-strike in that window. No TIER1-confirmed bilateral 48h exchange (strikes both ways) is documented June 10-21. The June 7-8 worst-strikes episode confirmed by CNN and TIER1 predates the F3 window. The Versailles MOU (June 17) established a 60-day ceasefire, suppressing the remaining risk of a qualifying bilateral exchange in the June 21-24 window. F3 is revised from 55% to 10% — the forecast stays ACTIVE until its June 24 horizon close; the expected resolution is NO. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The first nuclear-phase talks in Switzerland were scheduled to follow immediately after the MOU signing. Witkoff traveled toward Switzerland and Araghchi made preparations to join, with Qatar's PM al-Thani already in Burgenstock as mediator. On June 19, however, Israel struck numerous targets in southern Lebanon after four Israeli soldiers died in a Hezbollah attack, and Hezbollah struck Israeli positions at Kfar Tebnit. Vance canceled his Switzerland trip; Switzerland's Foreign Ministry confirmed the Burgenstock session was canceled. An anonymous US official said Israel and Hezbollah agreed to a renewed truce at 4 pm local time June 19, but IDF continued strikes after the deadline. The Switzerland postponement is the central open variable as of June 21. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Commercial shipping through Hormuz showed one day of partial recovery — 26 vessels transited June 18 as the US naval blockade lifted per MOU terms — before returning to near-zero June 19-21 as the Lebanon front disrupted ceasefire implementation and insurer confidence. Analysts at Lloyd's and Windward AI estimate 4+ months to full normalization; approximately 550 vessels remain stranded; mine-clearing operations (ongoing since April per CENTCOM) have not yet opened a cleared commercial lane. The MOU imposes a 30-day Iran obligation to reopen the strait and requires US blockade removal immediately, but enforcement mechanisms are unspecified and the Lebanon spoiler remains active. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>In Israeli domestic politics, a Times of Israel source reported mid-June that the Knesset dissolution bill is unlikely to pass its final readings and the October 27 mandatory election date is the expected outcome. No second or third reading has been scheduled; the parliamentary recess begins approximately July 16. Coalition negotiations over the election date (Netanyahu prefers October; Shas/UTJ prefer September) remain unresolved. The wartime ceasefire and active diplomatic track have, for now, reduced the immediate pressure on coalition partners to force an early dissolution — a wartime-consolidation pattern consistent with Israeli parliamentary precedent from 2006 and 2023-24. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

