      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Lebanon front re-ignites into a third full war, Iran suspends MOU compliance, Switzerland nuclear talks collapse, and Hormuz re-mined or blockaded — driven by F8 NO + Lebanon ceasefire failure + Ben-Gvir/Smotrich exit from coalition (F7 YES).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> The 60-day MOU holds but nuclear talks stall over enrichment red lines, Hormuz recovers only partially (F9 partial), F6 resolves NO (October 27 election on schedule), and the ceasefire remains shaky but intact — the most probable near-term state.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Switzerland nuclear talks convene (F8 YES), Iran's 30-day demining obligation produces a cleared commercial lane before July 17, Hormuz transit volume recovers toward F9 threshold, and Lebanese ceasefire holds — requiring all three tracks to stabilize simultaneously.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · June 10–21 · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media ran a bifurcated framing of the Islamabad MOU in the June 14-17 window. IRNA and IRIB foregrounded Pezeshkian's role as the sovereign who "secured Iran's core rights" and "preserved the enrichment program" — framing the MOU as a defensive victory rather than a concession. The 14-point text's deferral of nuclear program details to future negotiations was presented as Iran having "protected its positions" on enrichment. Press TV emphasized that the MOU contained no clause requiring Iran to surrender its HEU stockpile. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Tasnim, the IRGC-linked agency, adopted a sharper tone: it described leaked pre-signing drafts as "inaccurate" and "Western disinformation," and ran commentary questioning whether the Swiss nuclear talks would produce substantive concessions. Tasnim's coverage of the June 19 Lebanon fighting foregrounded Hezbollah casualties and IDF ceasefire violations, framing them as evidence that Israel "cannot be trusted as a partner in any settlement." This framing creates domestic political space for Araghchi to delay his Switzerland travel — the Hezbollah frame gives Iranian hardliners a credible pretext for non-compliance with the MOU timeline without formally rescinding the agreement. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan, the hardline outlet closest to the Supreme Leader's office, published an editorial on June 18 arguing that the MOU was "a 60-day ceasefire, not a peace agreement," and that Iran's leverage derived from the mines remaining in Hormuz — a position that, if it reflected leadership thinking, would run directly counter to the MOU's demining clause. No Supreme Leader statement on the MOU has appeared in IRNA or official Khamenei.ir as of June 21, an absence that Iranian analysts and Reuters noted as deliberate ambiguity about supreme-level endorsement. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

